1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,866
You do not need coding to build your first trading system.

2
00:00:09,315 --> 00:00:14,070
One Excel sheet, five clear rules, and a little patience.

3
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:19,040
Today we build a system from scratch, and backtest it too.

4
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:24,951
Hello, this is episode ten of the SPIDER Quant Trading series.

5
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:31,043
Last time: overfitting. When a backtest looks better than the truth.

6
00:00:31,493 --> 00:00:37,292
Today: your first system. Writing the rules, and a backtest in Excel.

7
00:00:38,292 --> 00:00:41,218
Every system answers five questions.

8
00:00:41,668 --> 00:00:46,109
What to trade? When to enter? When to admit you were wrong?

9
00:00:46,559 --> 00:00:53,324
When to exit? And how much to risk? Only when all five are written down beforehand is it a system.

10
00:00:54,324 --> 00:01:00,933
Our example: gold, daily chart. It is only for learning, not a tip.

11
00:01:01,383 --> 00:01:09,011
Entry: if today closes above the highest close of the last 20 days, buy at tomorrow's open.

12
00:01:09,461 --> 00:01:23,829
Stop: 2 ATR below entry. Exit: if the close falls below the lowest close of the last 10 days, out at the next open. Size: 1% risk per trade.

13
00:01:24,829 --> 00:01:27,833
How do you know a rule is clear?

14
00:01:28,283 --> 00:01:35,127
Give the same rules and the same chart to two different people. Their trades must come out exactly the same.

15
00:01:35,577 --> 00:01:42,969
"When the trend feels strong" does not work. "The highest close of 20 days" does.

16
00:01:43,969 --> 00:01:51,283
Now Excel. The first five columns: date, open, high, low, close.

17
00:01:51,734 --> 00:02:01,530
Then a column: the highest close of the last 20 days. Formula: MAX over the previous 20 rows. Today's row is not included.

18
00:02:01,979 --> 00:02:11,619
Likewise the lowest close of 10 days, with MIN. And an ATR column, the average daily range.

19
00:02:12,619 --> 00:02:19,541
Now the signal column. If today's close is above the 20-day column, write BUY.

20
00:02:19,991 --> 00:02:24,040
But the entry is not today. It is at the next row's open.

21
00:02:24,489 --> 00:02:31,882
Because today's close is only known when the day ends. Buying at that same close is buying with a look into the future.

22
00:02:32,882 --> 00:02:38,839
Look at one trade. Entry at 2400. ATR that day is 25.

23
00:02:39,289 --> 00:02:47,491
Stop 2 ATR below: 2350. So 1R equals 50.

24
00:02:47,941 --> 00:02:56,299
Exit at 2520. A 120 gain divided by 50: +2.4R.

25
00:02:57,299 --> 00:03:04,380
Now size. Say the account is 10,000 dollars. 1% is 100 dollars of risk.

26
00:03:04,829 --> 00:03:10,315
Risk per ounce is 50 dollars. So 100 divided by 50: 2 ounces.

27
00:03:10,765 --> 00:03:18,576
A wide stop means a small size, a tight stop a bigger one. The risk always stays 1%.

28
00:03:19,576 --> 00:03:28,249
Each trade is one row on a separate sheet: entry date, entry, stop, exit date, exit, and R.

29
00:03:28,698 --> 00:03:38,730
An example of 30 trades: 12 wins averaging +2.5R. 18 losses of −1R each.

30
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:50,647
In total +12R. Only 40% wins, yet a profit factor of 1.67 and an average of +0.4R per trade.

31
00:03:51,647 --> 00:03:54,155
Now apply the checks from earlier episodes.

32
00:03:54,605 --> 00:04:06,150
Costs: 0.1R per trade. 3R gone over 30 trades. Left: +9R, a profit factor near 1.45.

33
00:04:06,601 --> 00:04:14,228
And 30 trades is very few. Use hidden data, walk-forward, and other markets too. Only then trust it.

34
00:04:15,228 --> 00:04:17,737
Three common Excel mistakes.

35
00:04:18,187 --> 00:04:27,094
One: including today's row in the MAX. Then today's close can never beat its own max, and no signal ever comes.

36
00:04:27,544 --> 00:04:35,747
Two: forgetting costs. Three: running it once and then tweaking the rules. That is overfitting again.

37
00:04:36,747 --> 00:04:38,314
So, today's summary.

38
00:04:38,764 --> 00:04:47,437
A system is written answers to five questions: what, when in, stop, when out, how much.

39
00:04:47,887 --> 00:04:55,854
In Excel: the 20-day max, the 10-day min, ATR, the signal, and entry at the next open.

40
00:04:56,304 --> 00:05:01,712
Log every trade in R, subtract costs, and check on more data.

41
00:05:02,712 --> 00:05:08,197
Next episode: the same system in Python. Ten or twelve lines of code.

42
